1
00:00:04,729 --> 00:00:06,780
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:06,781 --> 00:00:13,260
This time, we'll figure out what your weaknesses are and learn a study method to match.

3
00:00:13,261 --> 00:00:18,821
Before you study, you need to know why you want to draw and what you're lacking.

4
00:00:19,025 --> 00:00:22,780
Let's look back at the process you've been using to draw.

5
00:00:23,290 --> 00:00:26,300
There are all sorts of reasons people draw.

6
00:00:26,301 --> 00:00:33,820
Since you're a classmate taking my class, I assume you're a concept artist or illustrator, or you want to become one.

7
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:37,860
So let me roughly split those goals into two.

8
00:00:37,861 --> 00:00:41,620
Becoming a concept artist, and becoming an illustrator.

9
00:00:43,255 --> 00:00:48,320
A concept artist's job is to convey a convincing design effectively.

10
00:00:48,321 --> 00:00:51,680
In the early stages of a project, they can also be the face of the team.

11
00:00:51,681 --> 00:01:00,041
Through concept art, you can grasp the overall mood of a game or film, and in the early stages you take on a role in all sorts of business avenues.

12
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:03,520
You can even raise investment with concept art alone.

13
00:01:03,521 --> 00:01:08,240
In other words, a single picture can be more convincing than a hundred words.

14
00:01:08,241 --> 00:01:14,570
But if your expressive ability is lacking, the difference in persuasiveness that comes from a picture's expressiveness is enormous.

15
00:01:15,184 --> 00:01:18,170
The same goes for an illustrator.

16
00:01:18,170 --> 00:01:23,850
I think of illustration as pushing a picture's communicative power to the maximum through all sorts of elements.

17
00:01:23,850 --> 00:01:27,250
That's why an illustrator's expressive ability matters even more.

18
00:01:27,834 --> 00:01:31,290
Because you have to convey every story you want to tell with a single picture.

19
00:01:34,934 --> 00:01:44,320
Since you have to pay attention to color, mood, design, composition, form, volume, and so many other elements, the importance of expressive ability grows even further.

20
00:01:45,739 --> 00:01:48,060
So what is expressive ability in drawing?

21
00:01:48,060 --> 00:01:54,860
You have to consider many elements, but I think accurate form and volume are the most important.

22
00:01:54,860 --> 00:01:58,540
Especially, photo-realistic-style drawing aims for a believable sense of three-dimensionality.

23
00:01:58,540 --> 00:02:01,460
People are used to seeing things three-dimensionally.

24
00:02:02,039 --> 00:02:05,340
That's because, since birth, we've seen all sorts of things in 3D.

25
00:02:06,249 --> 00:02:14,501
The more a picture has accurate form and looks three-dimensional, the more effectively it can convey the various stories within it.

26
00:02:14,970 --> 00:02:19,180
So what do you need to study to get accurate form and volume?

27
00:02:19,181 --> 00:02:25,020
Among the many aspiring artists I've observed, I found two big problems.

28
00:02:25,021 --> 00:02:27,940
The first is neglecting line drawing.

29
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:31,141
They try to hide the lack of brushwork skill behind nice-looking coloring.

30
00:02:31,369 --> 00:02:35,260
The second is passively accepting the instructor's lessons or materials.

31
00:02:39,350 --> 00:02:43,880
In drawing, I consider line drawing to be the most basic of basics.

32
00:02:43,881 --> 00:02:54,120
If you skip line drawing and work the so-called "paint-without-line-art" way, ask yourself whether you're really such a skilled concept artist or illustrator that you can skip the sketching stage.

33
00:02:54,121 --> 00:02:55,320
Most people are not.

34
00:02:55,885 --> 00:03:01,361
I've seen most people who draw this way are "making" a picture rather than "drawing" one.

35
00:03:01,644 --> 00:03:07,880
Ask yourself whether you're overusing Liquify, or cutting things up and gluing them together as you work.

36
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:13,720
Realistic light, color sense, and design all look very clumsy if the form falls apart.

37
00:03:13,721 --> 00:03:16,680
This is even more true for realistic-style pictures.

38
00:03:16,681 --> 00:03:19,320
Brushwork really shows in line drawing.

39
00:03:19,321 --> 00:03:23,960
If you want to draw well, practice building your form ability through line drawing.

40
00:03:23,961 --> 00:03:31,400
If your form reads accurately from the lines alone, consider yourself about fifty to seventy percent successful.

41
00:03:31,401 --> 00:03:34,360
Painting in shapes is the next step.

42
00:03:34,361 --> 00:03:40,340
In realistic-style drawing, the sketching process can be simpler than in animation drawing.

43
00:03:40,341 --> 00:03:46,340
By that I mean you don't have to cleanly ink the lines or sketch every accessory and pattern up front.

44
00:03:46,341 --> 00:03:49,620
This also applies to design.

45
00:03:49,621 --> 00:03:53,261
Draw big, draw simply, and draw accurately.

46
00:03:53,464 --> 00:03:58,100
Once line drawing is mastered, you can draw with shapes and no lines.

47
00:03:58,101 --> 00:04:02,900
But even then, sketching first gives you a much more finished result.

48
00:04:02,901 --> 00:04:06,420
Even if you start by painting shapes, it's better to add sketching as you go.

49
00:04:07,174 --> 00:04:13,520
Because the sharpness that lines convey is much easier to express than marking the boundary between one shape and another.

50
00:04:13,521 --> 00:04:17,161
The clearer the boundaries between objects, the cleaner a picture looks.

51
00:04:17,735 --> 00:04:20,880
That's why I sometimes intentionally leave lines in.

52
00:04:20,959 --> 00:04:30,121
This is especially true in concept art, and it's more effective there because it makes it easier for 3D modelers to understand the design.

53
00:04:31,480 --> 00:04:35,770
If you want to improve your drawing fast, you need to practice line drawing.

54
00:04:35,771 --> 00:04:43,290
I hope you become a concept artist or illustrator who can be communicative and expressive with line drawing alone.

55
00:04:43,291 --> 00:04:48,931
You shouldn't try to dazzle people with a picture smothered in flashy colors and brilliant light.

56
00:04:49,690 --> 00:04:52,940
So how should you study line drawing?

57
00:04:52,941 --> 00:04:54,700
First, draw things you like.

58
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:58,140
You need to get comfortable with your brush and tablet.

59
00:04:58,141 --> 00:05:02,140
A good brush someone gave me isn't necessarily a good brush for you.

60
00:05:02,141 --> 00:05:05,221
Try out lots of brushes and find the one that suits you.

61
00:05:05,795 --> 00:05:10,860
Even with the same brush, how it feels can change depending on how much you zoom the canvas.

62
00:05:10,861 --> 00:05:16,940
They say a craftsman doesn't blame his tools, but a brush that suits you makes drawing more enjoyable.

63
00:05:16,941 --> 00:05:17,941
Once you've found your brush,

64
00:05:18,030 --> 00:05:23,790
before you start the real picture, always loosen up your hand by doodling for at least ten minutes.

65
00:05:23,791 --> 00:05:26,750
I usually loosen my hand by drawing skulls.

66
00:05:26,751 --> 00:05:32,910
You could draw flowers, a puppy, or objects on your desk.

67
00:05:32,911 --> 00:05:35,630
You could also just scribble lines back and forth.

68
00:05:35,631 --> 00:05:40,830
At this stage you shouldn't worry about accuracy; just draw loosely as if you're playing.

69
00:05:41,630 --> 00:05:45,390
If you get stressed before you even start, you won't be able to begin drawing at all.

70
00:05:45,391 --> 00:05:47,150
Once you get used to it, try gesture drawing too.

71
00:05:47,860 --> 00:05:52,180
Sometimes I'll get really into drawing a picture, as if I'm finishing it with just a sketch.

72
00:05:52,905 --> 00:05:56,500
The important thing is that you get on friendly terms with line drawing.

73
00:05:56,501 --> 00:06:02,500
It would also be nice to build your own "home turf" where you can draw without hesitation even on a blank canvas.

74
00:06:02,501 --> 00:06:05,810
If coloring feels daunting, I recommend doing copy-studies of photos.

75
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:08,740
Do photo copy-studies, not picture copy-studies.

76
00:06:08,741 --> 00:06:11,300
A photo is the best teacher.

77
00:06:11,301 --> 00:06:16,340
Copy-study photos with all kinds of designs, light, people, and backgrounds.

78
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:21,360
Don't focus on drawing it identically; focus on understanding the principle.

79
00:06:21,361 --> 00:06:28,320
Usually people focus on mechanically copying it exactly, and after they're done, nothing stays in their head.

80
00:06:28,321 --> 00:06:34,640
Use the eyedropper on different parts of the photo, and observe how the value and saturation change.

81
00:06:34,641 --> 00:06:37,680
Also convert the photo to black and white to observe it.

82
00:06:37,681 --> 00:06:39,440
Let's observe the changes in value.

83
00:06:40,030 --> 00:06:47,901
Unless the light is intense at the edge and creates strong value contrast, value often doesn't change that much.

84
00:06:47,995 --> 00:06:50,820
Grasping inherent value is especially important in coloring.

85
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:56,420
If a picture looks muddy, check whether you used too little saturation in the shadows and midtones.

86
00:06:57,060 --> 00:07:00,770
It's good to make the saturation of the midtones a little higher than the lighter areas.

87
00:07:02,140 --> 00:07:06,190
When I was job hunting, I'd alternate between copy-studies and original work.

88
00:07:06,191 --> 00:07:14,670
The good thing about this is that copy-study reduces the pain of creating, and since the result looks much more complete than your actual skill, it boosts your confidence.

89
00:07:14,671 --> 00:07:17,710
That confidence can become the driving force for original work.

90
00:07:17,711 --> 00:07:26,030
The reason I do two copy-studies then one original is that copy-studies take less time and energy, so the time balances out.

91
00:07:26,031 --> 00:07:29,950
It's great to immediately apply the principles you learned from copy-study to your original work.

92
00:07:30,530 --> 00:07:33,851
The things you learn from copy-study finally become your own.

93
00:07:35,240 --> 00:07:40,961
So you think your sketching and coloring are solid, but your pictures aren't popular and you don't know your own style?

94
00:07:41,325 --> 00:07:43,560
Drawing isn't all about fundamentals.

95
00:07:43,561 --> 00:07:46,200
Fundamentals are, literally, the basics.

96
00:07:46,201 --> 00:07:49,400
If the basics aren't in place, you can't move forward.

97
00:07:49,401 --> 00:07:52,120
What else do you need beyond the fundamentals?

98
00:07:52,121 --> 00:07:56,120
You need creative elements like style and design ability.

99
00:07:56,121 --> 00:07:59,400
Design is especially important in concept art.

100
00:07:59,401 --> 00:08:02,600
Because it's a job where you're essentially drawing for the sake of design.

101
00:08:03,830 --> 00:08:06,520
Some people have a narrow sense of design.

102
00:08:06,521 --> 00:08:16,200
The trait of people with a narrow sense of design is that they only watch the movies or comics they like, or they only copy the designs of the instructor or the original artist they're studying from.

103
00:08:16,445 --> 00:08:18,680
These people need to broaden their horizons.

104
00:08:20,350 --> 00:08:24,560
Especially in the game industry, you should be exposed to lots of different films and games.

105
00:08:24,561 --> 00:08:27,840
Even if it's not your taste, think of it as study.

106
00:08:27,841 --> 00:08:30,681
It's much better than studying by reading books.

107
00:08:30,830 --> 00:08:34,841
In particular, get into popular comics, animation, films, and games.

108
00:08:35,580 --> 00:08:37,360
There's a reason something is popular.

109
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:40,480
Also analyze the art of popular artists.

110
00:08:40,481 --> 00:08:43,200
The important thing here is that I'm not saying to copy it identically.

111
00:08:43,580 --> 00:08:47,920
Don't imitate; reference it and make it your own.

112
00:08:47,921 --> 00:08:52,570
If it's a design, you can reference the silhouette or the color scheme.

113
00:08:52,571 --> 00:08:59,610
Also observe how they handle the viewer's eye through concentrating and scattering detail, so you can apply it to your own work.

114
00:08:59,611 --> 00:09:03,050
Drawing style is largely a matter of personal taste.

115
00:09:03,051 --> 00:09:09,770
As your expressive ability improves and you get good at drawing, your own style will naturally emerge.

116
00:09:09,771 --> 00:09:16,090
That's exactly why I emphasize fundamentals, and why you should draw because you want to.

117
00:09:16,091 --> 00:09:16,570
However.

118
00:09:16,670 --> 00:09:22,540
In concept art, which is a kind of commercial design, it's hard to push through with your own style.

119
00:09:22,541 --> 00:09:27,020
It has to be universal, popular, and utterly commercial.

120
00:09:27,021 --> 00:09:31,900
And in the vast majority of cases you have to follow the art director's established style.

121
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:38,501
If you want to find your own style, focus on practicing fundamentals so you can grow into many different styles.

122
00:09:41,815 --> 00:09:44,640
Everyone studies in different ways.

123
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:48,080
Some read books, some watch lectures on YouTube.

124
00:09:48,081 --> 00:09:50,480
Which medium you use doesn't matter.

125
00:09:50,481 --> 00:09:53,200
Who the teacher is matters less than this.

126
00:09:53,605 --> 00:09:59,680
What matters is not being passively fed information, but studying with you as the subject.

127
00:09:59,681 --> 00:10:11,440
If you saw a good photo and decided to copy-study it, rather than seeing the photo as flat, you should think about its three-dimensional structure and reflect on why the light and color influence each other.

128
00:10:11,980 --> 00:10:17,061
And you should draw while studying why the photographer shot it with that composition and color.

129
00:10:17,775 --> 00:10:33,800
If you study with books and videos, you should question the theories in them, test them yourself, observe with your own eyes, or draw them out, so you truly understand what the speaker means and organize your own thoughts.

130
00:10:33,801 --> 00:10:42,520
Furthermore, you should be able to take what you felt when you saw a great picture, photo, or design, and turn it into your own inspiration.

131
00:10:42,521 --> 00:10:45,841
We're creators who do creative work.

132
00:10:46,010 --> 00:10:50,300
The rote, passive study habits from your school days don't work.

133
00:10:50,301 --> 00:10:52,940
You always have to think creatively.

134
00:10:52,941 --> 00:10:56,060
I'm not going to impose my drawing style on you.

135
00:10:56,061 --> 00:11:01,020
I just hope the students who take this course don't become mass-produced copycats.

136
00:11:01,021 --> 00:11:05,660
Please develop what you learn in this class and make it your own.

137
00:11:06,060 --> 00:11:15,301
I hope you study in a way that you do it because you want to, in a way where you're the owner, so that what you study fully becomes yours.

138
00:11:15,620 --> 00:11:24,900
This time, we reminded ourselves of what we're lacking, and briefly looked at how to study drawing and how to work.

139
00:11:24,901 --> 00:11:28,420
Next time, we'll look at an efficient working process.
